//! gate — one tree, measured against a CLOSED vocabulary of gate legs, the
//! verdict returned as DATA with the tree's own head commit pinned to it.
//!
//! This is wave 1 of the codification (workflows/CODIFICATION-DESIGN.md §1.3):
//! the leaf workflow every other workflow invokes as a child. Its design
//! carries four laws structurally:
//!
//! CLOSED LEG VOCABULARY (class 1): a leg names a KIND from a fixed set, and
//! each kind maps to one whole-cloth command sealed in the worker document's
//! content hash. A caller can never choose which program runs — the language
//! refuses parameter-as-program — so "run this local script" is not writable.
//! An unknown kind becomes a NAMED red refusal probe in the results (a
//! terminal route cannot leave a sequence region), so the verdict stays red
//! and loud — a skipped leg is indistinguishable from a green leg, so no
//! kind is ever skipped.
//!
//! RC AS RECORDED RESULT (classes 4, 5): every leg comes back exit-zero-always
//! as a typed probe carrying its rc; the engine's history is the manifest.
//! There is no exit-file idiom to get wrong — a leg with no recorded probe
//! cannot advance the run.
//!
//! CONTENDED-RED-IS-NOT-RED (class 9): each probe carries a `contended` flag
//! the worker derives from the leg's own log (EMFILE, lld deaths). A
//! contended red re-runs, bounded by the caller's `contention_rounds` and the
//! language's static visit ceiling, and every rerun is a recorded visit —
//! classification is step policy in history, never judgment in a chat log.
//!
//! VERDICT IS DATA (aion#58): a red gate is a SUCCESSFUL measurement. The
//! failure routes are reserved for could-not-measure faces: an unknown leg
//! kind, a tree that is not there. `measured.green` is the parent's to route
//! on; `head_commit` pins exactly what tree the verdict is about.
//!
//! CONTENDED GREEN DOES NOT RERUN (wave-1 decision, stated): only a
//! contended RED re-runs. A contended GREEN keeps its flag in the probe the
//! parent receives — the timing-verdicts law says the contended green is the
//! dangerous one, so the flag travels as data and the land workflow's battery
//! spec routes on `contended` greens rather than this workflow quietly
//! re-running them.
//!
//! LOGS LIVE IN THE TREE at `<tree>/.gate/` — the tree is the blackboard.
//! OBLIGATION ON EVERY LANDING CONSUMER: `.gate/` never rides a landing
//! commit; the land workflow stages by explicit pathspec and carries this
//! exclusion by name.
//!
//! SALVAGE IS AN INPUT MODE (design delta b): `tree` may point at any kept
//! tree, and `provenance` travels with the verdict naming how the tree came
//! to be gated (fresh flight, salvage of a kept tree, landing candidate).
//! `lifecycle` is the arc correlation handle (aion#77 interim convention).
workflow gate
  timeout 8h
  input tree: String
  input provenance: String
  input lifecycle: String
  input legs: [LegSpec]
  // The maximum contended re-runs PER LEG (>= 1 — it is the language's visit
  // bound on the rerun cycle, and a bound must be a real bound; a caller who
  // wants no reruns states 1 and routes on the contended flag instead).
  input contention_rounds: Int
  outcome measured:      type Measured, route success
  outcome unprovisioned: type NoTree,   route failure

/// One requested leg. `kind` selects from the closed vocabulary; the other
/// fields parameterize the kinds that need them and are ignored by the kinds
/// that do not (stated explicitly: `crate_name` feeds clippy_crate,
/// test_crate_lib, and lock_witness; `jobs` feeds the workspace kinds;
/// `pattern` feeds lock_witness as the version-regex refused in the lock).
type LegSpec { kind: String, crate_name: String, jobs: Int, pattern: String }

/// One measured leg: the kind echoed back, the rc as recorded fact, the
/// contention classification derived from the leg's own log, and the log's
/// path in the tree (the tree is the blackboard; the log is the evidence).
type LegProbe {
  kind: String,
  green: Bool,
  rc: Int,
  contended: Bool,
  log_path: String,
  refusal: String,
}

/// The tree's identity probe: refusal as data, and the head commit that pins
/// every verdict this run emits to the exact tree it measured.
type TreeProbe { ok: Bool, refusal: String, head_commit: String }

/// The measurement, whole: green is the conjunction over legs, and the probes
/// travel so the parent can route on any leg's own rc, read any log at its
/// path, and cite the head commit the verdict is about.
type Measured {
  green: Bool,
  head_commit: String,
  legs: [LegProbe],
  provenance: String,
  lifecycle: String,
}

/// The tree was not there to measure.
type NoTree { refusal: String, tree: String }

worker gate_ops
  /// Identify the tree: exists, is a git work tree, name its HEAD.
  action probe_tree(tree: String) -> TreeProbe
    timeout 5m, retry 2 every 30s
  /// Full workspace test suite at bounded parallelism.
  action run_test_workspace(tree: String, kind: String, jobs: Int) -> LegProbe
    timeout 4h, retry 2 every 30s
  /// Workspace clippy wall, all targets, warnings denied.
  action run_clippy_workspace(tree: String, kind: String, jobs: Int) -> LegProbe
    timeout 2h, retry 2 every 30s
  /// One crate's clippy wall (the feature-resolution twin of the workspace
  /// run — both are required because `--workspace` and `-p` resolve features
  /// differently; the caller states both legs).
  action run_clippy_crate(tree: String, kind: String, crate_name: String) -> LegProbe
    timeout 2h, retry 2 every 30s
  /// One crate's lib unit population.
  action run_test_crate_lib(tree: String, kind: String, crate_name: String) -> LegProbe
    timeout 2h, retry 2 every 30s
  /// Formatting check (measurement only; the autoformat law lives in the
  /// authoring loop, not in the gate).
  action run_fmt_check(tree: String, kind: String) -> LegProbe
    timeout 30m, retry 2 every 30s
  /// Refuse a leg kind outside the closed vocabulary — as a NAMED red probe
  /// (green: false, refusal naming the accepted set), because a terminal
  /// route cannot leave a sequence region and a silently skipped leg would
  /// read as green. The refusal probe keeps the verdict red and loud.
  action refuse_leg(kind: String) -> LegProbe
    timeout 5m, retry 2 every 30s
  /// The lock witness: a dependency floor is proven at the RESOLVED LOCK,
  /// never at a manifest line — rows matching the refused-version pattern
  /// red the leg (a patch is witnessed at the lock).
  action run_lock_witness(tree: String, kind: String, crate_name: String, pattern: String) -> LegProbe
    timeout 5m, retry 2 every 30s

/// The tree must exist and identify itself before any verdict can be about it.
step provision_check
  probe_tree(tree: tree) -> tree_probe
  outcome ok: when tree_probe.ok, route open
  outcome missing: otherwise,
    route unprovisioned(refusal: tree_probe.refusal, tree: tree)

/// One leg at a time, in the caller's order — gate legs contend for the same
/// box, so the sequence region is the honest shape.
step open
  sequence leg in legs

/// Select the leg's action from the closed vocabulary. An unknown kind
/// routes to the refusal action: a named red probe in the results, because
/// a skipped leg is indistinguishable from a green one and a terminal route
/// cannot leave the region.
step dispatch after open
  outcome a: when leg.kind == "test_workspace", route leg_test_workspace
  outcome b: when leg.kind == "clippy_workspace", route leg_clippy_workspace
  outcome c: when leg.kind == "clippy_crate", route leg_clippy_crate
  outcome d: when leg.kind == "test_crate_lib", route leg_test_crate_lib
  outcome e: when leg.kind == "fmt_check", route leg_fmt_check
  outcome f: when leg.kind == "lock_witness", route leg_lock_witness
  outcome unknown: otherwise, route leg_unknown

step leg_unknown
  refuse_leg(kind: leg.kind) -> probe
  route settle

step leg_test_workspace
  run_test_workspace(tree: tree, kind: leg.kind, jobs: leg.jobs) -> probe
  route settle

step leg_clippy_workspace
  run_clippy_workspace(tree: tree, kind: leg.kind, jobs: leg.jobs) -> probe
  route settle

step leg_clippy_crate
  run_clippy_crate(tree: tree, kind: leg.kind, crate_name: leg.crate_name) -> probe
  route settle

step leg_test_crate_lib
  run_test_crate_lib(tree: tree, kind: leg.kind, crate_name: leg.crate_name) -> probe
  route settle

step leg_fmt_check
  run_fmt_check(tree: tree, kind: leg.kind) -> probe
  route settle

step leg_lock_witness
  run_lock_witness(tree: tree, kind: leg.kind, crate_name: leg.crate_name, pattern: leg.pattern) -> probe
  route settle

/// A contended red re-runs, bounded by the caller's `contention_rounds`
/// (>= 1; the visit bound is this input, per-LEG: each sequence item lowers
/// to its own nested flow whose entry wrapper seeds the visit counter to
/// zero — emitter/flowshape.rs rewrite 2, emitter/flows.rs emit_wrapper — so
/// one leg's passages never consume another's budget). Every rerun is a
/// recorded visit, and the rerun's probe REPLACES the contended one: route-
/// cycle re-entry rebinding is one of the checker's two sanctioned
/// single-assignment exceptions, and collect binds ONE slot per input item
/// in item order (reference §8) — a contended-then-green leg contributes its
/// green probe, a contended-then-red leg its red one; contention never
/// launders a genuine red.
step settle
  outcome rerun: when probe.contended and not probe.green and visits < contention_rounds,
    route dispatch
  outcome next: otherwise, route close
  max 9 visits

/// Close the region: every leg's probe, in the caller's order.
step close
  collect probe -> probes
  route verdict

/// The verdict: green is the conjunction over every collected probe, and it
/// travels WITH the probes and the head commit — a parent routes on the
/// verdict but cites the evidence.
step verdict
  probes |> all(.green) -> all_green
  outcome green: when all_green,
    route measured(green: true, head_commit: tree_probe.head_commit, legs: probes,
      provenance: provenance, lifecycle: lifecycle)
  outcome red: otherwise,
    route measured(green: false, head_commit: tree_probe.head_commit, legs: probes,
      provenance: provenance, lifecycle: lifecycle)
